1. Expert-witnesses shall have the following rights and obligations:
a) Read documents in the lawsuit files which are related to the to be-expertised objects; petition Courts to provide documents necessary for the expertise;

b) Question participants in legal procedures about matters related to the to be - expertised object
c) Be present under Courts' written summons; present, explain and answer questions related to the expertise and expertise conclusions in an honest, well-grounded and objective manner;

d) Notify the Courts in writing that the expertise is impossible as the matters needed to be expertised go beyond their professional capability and/or the documents supplied in service of the expertise are inadequate or unusable;

dd) Preserve the received documents and return them to Courts together with their expertise conclusions or with the notices that the expertise is impossible;

e) Not to arbitrarily collect materials for conducting the expertise nor to contact other participants in the procedures if such contact effects the expertise results; not to disclose secret information they know while conducting the expertise nor to notify the expertise results to other persons, except for the Judges who decide to request the expertise;
g) Be paid related expenses according to law regulations;

h) Make commitments before Courts to perform their rights and obligations.

2. Expert-witnesses shall refuse to conduct the expertise or be replaced in the following cases:

a) One of the cases prescribed in clauses 1 and 3 of Article 52 of this Code or Article 34 of the Law on Judicial Expertise;
b) They have participated in the procedures in the capacity as defense counsels of the legitimate rights and interests of the involved parties, as witnesses or interpreters in the same lawsuit;

