4. Deadline for handover of materials and evidences shall be defined by the Judge in charge of the cases provided that it does not exceed the duration of preparation for adjudication according to first-instance procedures and/or the duration of preparation for resolution of civil matters according to regulations in this Code.
If the involved parties fail to hand over materials and evidences required by the Courts due to good and sufficient reasons and they hand over such materials and evidences after decisions to bring the cases to trials according to first-instance procedures and decisions to hold meetings to resolve the civil matters are issued, such involved parties shall prove the reasons for the lateness of handover of these materials/evidences. In case where materials and evidences are not required to be handed over to the Courts by the involved parties and the involved parties cannot know about such materials and evidences during the resolution of the cases according to first-instance procedures, the involved parties may hand over and present such materials/evidences in first- instance trial sessions or the meetings for resolution of civil matters or later procedural stages of the resolution of such civil cases.

5. When the involved parties hand over materials and evidences to the Courts, copies of such materials and evidences shall be sent to other involved parties or their lawful representatives;
regarding materials and evidences specified in clause 2 Article 109 of this Code or materials and evidences whose copies cannot be made, written notifications must be sent to other involved parties or their lawful representatives.

Article 97. Verification and collection of evidences

1. Agencies, organizations and individuals may collect materials and evidences themselves by taking the following measures:
a) Collecting readable, audible or visible materials, electronic data;

b) Collecting exhibits;

c) Defining witnesses and collecting confirmation of the witnesses;

