3. Courts shall make property valuation decisions and set up Valuation Councils in the following cases:
a) At the request of an involved party or involved parties;

b) The involved parties fail to reach agreement on selection of property price appraisal organizations or offer different prices or fail to reach agreement on property price;

c) The parties reach agreements with each other or with property price appraisal organizations at the prices which are lower than the market prices of the area where the property is located at the time of valuation to evade fulfilling obligations to the State or the third parties or there are grounds proving that the property price appraisal organizations commit violations against regulations of law when conducting price appraisal.
4. Procedures for establishment of Valuation Councils and procedures for property valuation:

a) Valuation Councils shall be established by Courts and consist of the representatives of financial agencies, acting as the Chairpersons and representatives of relevant specialized agencies, acting as members. Persons who have conducted procedures in such lawsuits and persons specified in Article 52 of this Code must not be members of Valuation Councils.

Valuation Councils shall conduct valuation only when no members of the Councils are absent.
When it is necessary, representatives of People’s Committees of communes where the to be- valuated properties are located shall be invited to witness the valuation. Involved parties shall be priorly notified of time and place of valuation, shall be entitled to attend and express opinions about the valuation. Valuation Councils shall be entitled to make decisions on prices of the properties to be valued;

