b) Medicine sent by a relative for a person held in temporary custody or temporary detention shall be entered in the register of medicines sent by relatives, stored in a separate cabinet, and inspected and managed by a health official of the detention facility. When the person held in temporary custody or temporary detention needs to use the medicine, the health official shall, based on the medical condition and treatment regimen, administer the medicine or instruct and closely supervise that person in taking it. The person receiving and taking the medicine shall sign or fingerprint the register of medicines sent by relatives. Expired medicine shall be destroyed under minutes, in the presence of the person held in temporary custody or temporary detention, who shall sign or fingerprint the minutes in confirmation;

c) Upon the release of a person held in temporary custody or temporary detention, the health official shall inspect and reconcile the records and return any unused medicine to that person. In the case of transfer to another detention facility, the health official shall hand over the medicine to the receiving facility. If the person held in temporary custody or temporary detention dies or is executed under a death sentence, the health official shall hand over the medicine to his/her relative or authorized person.

2. Where a person held in temporary custody or temporary detention shows signs of a mental illness or another disease causing loss of the capacity to perceive or control his/her acts, the detention facility shall notify and request the case-handling agency to solicit a forensic psychiatric assessment. Within 3 working days from receipt of the request from the detention facility, the case-handling agency shall consider and decide, within its jurisdiction, whether to solicit a forensic psychiatric assessment. If no assessment is solicited, the agency shall provide a written reply specifying the reason.

